#X text 240 83 GPIO pins are taken into exclusive ownership: only one
entity can read or set the value of a pin. Send the [release( message
to relinquish ownership of the pin.;
#X obj 230 88 cnv 1 2 45 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -221520 -1 0;
#X text 240 138 That is \, you can create any number of [hw.rpi.gpio]
objects \, but access to a specific pin will be available only to one
of them (the first one that captured this pin).;
#X obj 230 143 cnv 1 2 60 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 240 208 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 233 • Sensor input — reading digital values from buttons
\, switches \, motion sensors or other on/off peripherals connected
to the Raspberry Pi GPIO header \, with optional debouncing via the
poll method.;
#X text 250 303 • Actuator control — driving LEDs \, relays \, motors
and other output devices from a patch \, including single pulses via
impulse and continuous control via software PWM (soft_pwm / soft_pwm_freq).;
